"executive_summary": "This paper investigates whether giant planets can form close to solar-type stars through gravitational fragmentation of protoplanetary discs. Two competing theories of giant planet formation are core accretion and gravitational fragmentation. Core accretion is slow, requiring millions of years, which may exceed the lifetime of circumstellar discs. Gravitational fragmentation is fast, occurring on a dynamical timescale of a few hundred years, but requires two conditions: gravitational instability (Toomre Q < 1) and fast cooling (tCOOL < C(γ) tORB). The authors use radiative hydrodynamic simulations with a newly developed method that accounts for radiative transfer effects, including the equation of state and energy equation, in smoothed particle hydrodynamics (SPH) simulations. They simulate a 0.07 M⊙ disc around a 0.5 M⊙ star, extending from 2 to 40 AU, in two cases: one with a constant background radiation field of 10 K, and one with additional heating from the central star. In both cases, the disc does not fragment. In the first case, the disc cannot cool fast enough (cooling time > 2 tORB). In the second case, the disc is not gravitationally unstable (Q > 1.5). The results corroborate previous studies by Boley et al. (2006) and Cai et al. (2007), who used different radiative transfer treatments. The authors conclude that disc fragmentation is unlikely to produce giant planets around solar-type stars at radii < 50 AU. This work contributes to resolving the contradictory results in the literature about whether disc fragmentation can produce giant planets close to the central star, which stem from different treatments of radiative transfer. The study uses realistic dust opacities and an equation of state that includes the rotational and vibrational degrees of freedom of molecular hydrogen, as well as the chemical states of hydrogen and helium. The simulations are performed using the SPH code dragon, which uses an octal tree to compute gravity and find neighbours, adaptive smoothing lengths, multiple particle timesteps, artificial viscosity with the Balsara switch, and a second-order Runge-Kutta integration scheme. The authors also compare their results with analytic calculations of Hubeny (1990) for the vertical temperature profile of the disc. The study is important because it provides evidence that gravitational fragmentation is not a viable mechanism for forming giant planets close to the central star, which has implications for our understanding of planet formation and the diversity of exoplanets. The authors acknowledge that their results are consistent with previous simulations and analytical calculations, which suggests that the conclusion is robust. However, they also note that fragmentation may be possible at larger radii (> 100 AU), where the cooling times are sufficiently short, but that such objects are unlikely to migrate inwards to become hot Jupiters. The study is limited by the assumption of a constant mean molecular weight and the neglect of metal contributions, which may affect the accuracy of the results. Overall, this paper provides a comprehensive investigation of the possibility of giant planet formation by gravitational fragmentation, using state-of-the-art numerical simulations and realistic physical models.",
